        "description": "<div id=\"link_wrapped_content\">\n<body><section class=\"wp-block-bigbite-multi-title\"><div class=\"container\"></div></section>\n\n\n\n<p>AMD\u2019s V-Xache-infused X3D series of processors has been an absolute smash hit in the PC gaming space, pushing AMD to the forefront of the performance discussion after years of Intel dominance. But after three generations of success, fans were probably wondering when AMD would pull out all the stops for a new design. Well the Ryzen 9 9950X3D2 Dual Edition, the absolute pinnacle of consumer desktop performance, is here\u2026and you probably shouldn\u2019t buy it.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>That sounds pessimistic. But I have to give credit to AMD here: The company <a href=\"https://www.pcworld.com/article/3099764/the-new-ryzen-9-did-amd-just-announce-a-cpu-for-exactly-no-one.html\"><em>isn\u2019t </em>marketing this chip to PC gamers</a>. And it easily could have, taking advantage of a bit of consumer confusion to sell a flagship product to people who don\u2019t need it. But AMD has been very clea",
